-- Q1: easy / L1 — Basic EPS formula
(2, 'Equity', 'What formula is used to calculate basic earnings per share (EPS)?', '["(Net income − Preferred dividends) ÷ Weighted-average common shares outstanding", "Net income ÷ Total shares authorized", "(Net income + Interest expense) ÷ Diluted shares outstanding", "Net income ÷ Shares outstanding at year-end"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45, basic EPS equals net income minus preferred dividends divided by weighted-average common shares outstanding. Preferred dividends are subtracted because they represent income unavailable to common shareholders. Option D is incorrect because EPS uses the weighted-average shares throughout the period, not the year-end balance, to reflect the time shares were actually outstanding.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — OCI vs AOCI distinction
(2, 'Equity', 'Under ASC 220, what is the distinction between other comprehensive income (OCI) and accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI)?', '["OCI includes net income while AOCI excludes net income", "OCI is reported on the balance sheet while AOCI appears on the income statement", "OCI is the current-period change while AOCI is the cumulative equity balance on the balance sheet", "OCI applies only to foreign currency adjustments while AOCI covers all equity items"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, OCI represents the current-period items that bypass net income (unrealized gains/losses on AFS securities, foreign currency translations, pension adjustments, hedge gains/losses). AOCI is the running cumulative total of all prior OCI items, reported as a component of stockholders'' equity on the balance sheet. Option A is incorrect because OCI specifically excludes net income — comprehensive income equals net income plus OCI.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as liability (ASC 480)
(2, 'Equity', 'Thornfield Industries issues 10,000 shares of preferred stock with a mandatory redemption feature requiring the company to repurchase all shares at $110 per share on December 31, 2030. Under ASC 480, how should this instrument be classified on the balance sheet?', '["As permanent equity within stockholders'' equity", "As a liability because it embodies an unconditional obligation to transfer assets", "As mezzanine equity between liabilities and stockholders'' equity", "As temporary equity until the mandatory redemption date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable financial instruments must be classified as liabilities because they embody an unconditional obligation to transfer assets at a specified or determinable date. The $1,100,000 redemption amount is reported as a liability, not equity. Option A is incorrect because permanent equity classification is reserved for instruments without mandatory redemption features — the obligatory repurchase removes the residual-interest characteristic of equity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Retained earnings analysis with multiple equity events
(2, 'Equity', 'Meridian Corp. begins 2025 with retained earnings of $2,400,000. During the year it reports net income of $600,000, declares cash dividends of $200,000, declares a 10% stock dividend (50,000 new shares at $25 market price and $5 par), discovers a $75,000 prior-period error that overstated prior-year revenue, and records a $40,000 unrealized gain on AFS debt securities. What is the ending retained earnings balance?', '["$1,475,000", "$1,515,000", "$1,550,000", "$2,475,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 250-10-45 and ASC 505-20-30, retained earnings is affected by net income (+$600,000), cash dividends (−$200,000), the stock dividend at fair value (−$1,250,000 = 50,000 × $25), and the prior-period adjustment (−$75,000). The AFS unrealized gain ($40,000) bypasses retained earnings and goes to AOCI under ASC 320. Ending RE = $2,400,000 − $75,000 + $600,000 − $200,000 − $1,250,000 = $1,475,000. Option B ($1,515,000) incorrectly adds the AFS gain to retained earnings instead of AOCI.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Cash-settled SARs liability classification and Year 1 expense
(2, 'Equity', 'On January 1, Year 1, Vanguard Corp. grants 6,000 stock appreciation rights (SARs) to executives that must be settled in cash. The SARs cliff-vest after three years. At the grant date, each SAR has a fair value of $12. At December 31, Year 1, the fair value increases to $15 per SAR. Under ASC 718, what compensation expense should Vanguard recognize in Year 1?', '["$24,000", "$36,000", "$15,000", "$30,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 718-30-35, cash-settled SARs are liability-classified awards that are remeasured at fair value each reporting period. Year 1 cumulative expense = 6,000 SARs × $15 current fair value × 1/3 vesting fraction = $30,000. Unlike equity-classified awards where fair value is locked at grant date, liability awards use the updated fair value at each reporting date. Option A ($24,000) incorrectly uses the $12 grant-date fair value, which would apply only to equity-classified awards under ASC 718-10-30.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Liquidating dividend definition
(2, 'Equity', 'A corporation distributes assets to shareholders in excess of its accumulated retained earnings. Under U.S. GAAP, this type of distribution is referred to as:', '["A regular cash dividend from current-period earnings", "A liquidating dividend representing a return of invested capital", "A stock dividend reducing the par value of outstanding shares", "A property dividend measured at the asset''s carrying amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A liquidating dividend is a distribution to shareholders that exceeds accumulated earnings, representing a return of contributed capital rather than profits. Under ASC 505-20, the recipient reduces the investment account rather than recognizing dividend income. Option A is incorrect because liquidating dividends specifically exceed retained earnings — any dividend returning capital qualifies regardless of the company''s financial condition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Legal capital impairment test restricts dividend amount
(2, 'Equity', 'Hallmark Corp. has common stock ($10 par, 50,000 shares) of $500,000, APIC of $300,000, and retained earnings of $180,000. State law prohibits dividend declarations that would reduce stockholders'' equity below aggregate par value. What is the maximum cash dividend Hallmark may declare?', '["$180,000", "$300,000", "$480,000", "$980,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under state corporate law (e.g., RMBCA), legal capital restrictions prohibit distributions that reduce equity below aggregate par value. Hallmark''s par value floor is $500,000. Total equity is $980,000, so maximum dividends = $980,000 − $500,000 = $480,000. Option A ($180,000) assumes only retained earnings are distributable, but legal capital tests based on par value allow distributions from APIC when state law defines legal capital as par value only.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Stock option exercise journal entry (APIC reclassification)
(2, 'Equity', 'Brentwood Corp. granted 5,000 stock options to employees with an exercise price of $20 per share. Over the three-year vesting period, the company recognized total compensation expense of $75,000, credited to APIC — Stock Options. On the exercise date, all 5,000 options are exercised when the market price is $38. The common stock has a $1 par value. What is the credit to APIC — Common Stock upon exercise?', '["$95,000", "$100,000", "$185,000", "$170,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 718-10-35, upon exercise the entity debits Cash ($100,000 = 5,000 × $20) and debits APIC — Stock Options ($75,000 reclassification), then credits Common Stock at par ($5,000 = 5,000 × $1) and APIC — Common Stock for the remainder ($170,000). The key is recognizing that both cash proceeds and the accumulated APIC — Stock Options flow into APIC — Common Stock. Option A ($95,000) excludes the reclassification of APIC — Stock Options.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Expired vested options — no income reversal
(2, 'Equity', 'Under ASC 718, what happens when vested employee stock options expire unexercised?', '["The APIC — Stock Options balance remains in equity and is not reversed", "Previously recognized compensation expense is reversed through net income", "The entity records a gain on the income statement", "APIC — Stock Options is reclassified to retained earnings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 718-10-35-8, when vested stock options expire unexercised, the accumulated APIC — Stock Options balance remains in equity. No adjustment to compensation expense or net income is recorded because the expense was for the service received, regardless of exercise. Option B is incorrect because reversing the expense would contradict the principle that compensation was earned through the employee''s requisite service period.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — Constructive retirement method vs cost method for treasury stock
(2, 'Equity', 'Waverly Corp. reacquires 3,000 shares of its $5 par common stock at $28 per share. The shares were originally issued at $20 per share. The board does not intend to reissue the shares. If Waverly uses the constructive retirement method instead of the cost method, how does the entry differ at reacquisition?', '["The constructive retirement method debits Treasury Stock for the full reacquisition cost of $84,000", "The constructive retirement method removes par value and original APIC, charges the excess to retained earnings, and uses no treasury stock account", "Both methods produce identical journal entries at the reacquisition date regardless of intent", "The constructive retirement method records shares at par value and credits APIC for any reacquisition premium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the constructive retirement method, shares are treated as permanently retired at reacquisition: Common Stock is debited at par ($15,000), APIC at the original excess ($45,000), and Retained Earnings absorbs the $24,000 difference between the $28 reacquisition cost and $20 original issue price. No Treasury Stock account is used. Option A describes the cost method, which records a single debit to Treasury Stock at the $84,000 reacquisition cost under ASC 505-30-30.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Share-based payment liability vs equity classification (employee settlement choice)
(2, 'Equity', 'Lakefield Corp. grants employees an award that can be settled in either cash or shares at the employees'' option. Under ASC 718, how should this award be classified on the balance sheet?', '["As equity because shares may be issued at settlement", "As mezzanine equity until the settlement method is determined", "As a liability because the employee''s cash alternative creates an unavoidable obligation", "As equity until the employee formally elects cash settlement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 718-10-25-11, when an employee can elect cash settlement, the award is classified as a liability because the entity cannot avoid a potential cash outflow. The entity must remeasure the liability at fair value each reporting period until settlement. Option A is incorrect because equity classification requires that the entity — not the employee — controls whether settlement occurs in shares. Only when the employer holds the settlement choice can it classify the award as equity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Graded vesting accelerated attribution method (ASC 718)
(2, 'Equity', 'Bridgewater Inc. grants 9,000 stock options on January 1, Year 1, with graded vesting: 3,000 vest after Year 1, 3,000 after Year 2, and 3,000 after Year 3. The grant-date fair value is $8 per option. Bridgewater elects the accelerated attribution method under ASC 718. What is compensation expense in Year 1?', '["$44,000", "$24,000", "$36,000", "$72,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 718-10-35-8, the accelerated attribution method treats each vesting tranche as a separate award. Tranche 1 (3,000 × $8 over 1 year): $24,000. Tranche 2 (3,000 × $8 over 2 years): $12,000 in Year 1. Tranche 3 (3,000 × $8 over 3 years): $8,000 in Year 1. Total Year 1 = $44,000. Option B ($24,000) uses straight-line over 3 years ($72,000 ÷ 3), the alternative approach that produces lower first-year expense and higher expense in later years.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Retained earnings restriction disclosure
(2, 'Equity', 'Harborview Inc. has total retained earnings of $4,000,000. A bond indenture restricts $1,500,000 from dividend distribution. How should this restriction be reported?', '["Deducted from total retained earnings on the balance sheet", "Disclosed in the notes to the financial statements", "Reported as a separate liability on the balance sheet", "Recorded as an appropriation reducing total stockholders'' equity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 505-10-50-3, restrictions on retained earnings imposed by loan agreements or other contracts are disclosed in the notes to the financial statements. The restriction does not reduce retained earnings or create a liability — it simply limits the amount available for dividends. Option C is incorrect because a restriction on distributions is not an obligation to transfer assets; it is a contractual limitation on how existing equity may be used.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Comprehensive income computation (classify NI vs OCI items)
(2, 'Equity', 'Pinnacle Corp. reports net income of $500,000, unrealized gain on AFS debt securities of $35,000, foreign currency translation loss of $12,000, realized gain on sale of equipment of $20,000, and pension prior service cost arising during the period of $8,000. What is total comprehensive income?', '["$535,000", "$523,000", "$545,000", "$515,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, comprehensive income = net income + OCI. OCI items: AFS unrealized gain (+$35,000), foreign currency translation loss (−$12,000), pension prior service cost (−$8,000) = +$15,000. The equipment sale gain ($20,000) is already in net income, not a separate OCI item. Total = $500,000 + $15,000 = $515,000. Option A ($535,000) incorrectly adds the equipment gain as a separate OCI component, double-counting it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — ASC 718 modification incremental fair value
(2, 'Equity', 'On January 1, Year 1, Kelton Corp. grants an employee 4,000 stock options with a grant-date fair value of $10 per option and a four-year vesting period. After two years ($20,000 recognized), the company reduces the exercise price. On the modification date, the original options have a fair value of $6 and the modified options have a fair value of $14. What is total compensation expense recognized over the full four-year period?', '["$72,000", "$56,000", "$40,000", "$52,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 718-20-35-3, total compensation after modification equals the original grant-date fair value ($40,000 = 4,000 × $10) plus incremental fair value at modification ($32,000 = 4,000 × ($14 − $6)). The incremental cost is recognized over the remaining two years alongside the original amortization. Total = $40,000 + $32,000 = $72,000. Option B ($56,000) uses only the modified fair value (4,000 × $14) without properly combining original and incremental amounts.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Identifying which items bypass net income and go to OCI
(2, 'Equity', 'Prescott International reports sales revenue of $5,000,000, COGS of $3,200,000, an unrealized holding gain on a designated cash flow hedge of $45,000, gain on sale of a building of $120,000, and a foreign currency translation adjustment loss of $28,000. Which amounts are reported in other comprehensive income?', '["The $45,000 hedge gain and the $120,000 building gain", "The $45,000 hedge gain, the $28,000 translation loss, and the $120,000 building gain", "The $45,000 hedge gain and the $28,000 translation loss only", "The $28,000 translation loss only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, OCI captures items that bypass net income: unrealized gains/losses on cash flow hedges and foreign currency translation adjustments. The $45,000 hedge gain and $28,000 translation loss are OCI items. The building sale gain ($120,000) is a realized transaction included in net income. Option B incorrectly classifies the realized building gain as OCI — gains on asset disposals always flow through the income statement under ASC 360.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Contingently issuable shares in diluted EPS
(2, 'Equity', 'Orion Corp. has a contingent share agreement to issue 30,000 additional common shares if annual revenue exceeds $10,000,000. For 2025, Orion reports revenue of $11,200,000, net income of $2,300,000, and 500,000 weighted-average common shares with no preferred stock. Under ASC 260, how should the contingently issuable shares affect diluted EPS?', '["Excluded because actual share issuance has not yet occurred", "Included in the diluted EPS denominator because the revenue threshold is satisfied at year-end", "Included only if management projects the revenue condition will persist in future years", "Deducted from the numerator as potential dilution cost rather than added to the denominator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS when the contingency condition is satisfied at the reporting date. Since Orion''s $11,200,000 revenue exceeds the $10,000,000 threshold, the 30,000 shares are added to the denominator as if outstanding from the beginning of the period, yielding diluted EPS of $2,300,000 ÷ 530,000 = $4.34. Option A is incorrect because inclusion depends on whether the condition is currently met, not whether shares have been physically issued.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Par value / legal capital definition
(2, 'Equity', 'What does par value represent in the context of a corporation''s equity?', '["The minimum legal capital per share established in the corporate charter", "The current market value of the stock on the date of issuance", "The amount of retained earnings allocated to each share", "The price at which treasury stock must be reacquired"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Par value is an arbitrary per-share amount assigned in the corporate charter that establishes the legal capital of the corporation. It has no relationship to market value and serves primarily as a legal minimum below which shares generally cannot be issued under ASC 505-10-45. Option B is incorrect because par value is typically nominal (e.g., $1 or $0.01) and bears no relation to the stock''s market price at issuance or any other date.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Comprehensive income with reclassification adjustment
(2, 'Equity', 'Pendleton Corp. begins the year with AOCI of $120,000. During the year, the company records $25,000 of unrealized gains on AFS debt securities and reclassifies $18,000 of previously unrealized gains to net income upon selling the securities. Net income is $400,000. What is total comprehensive income?', '["$425,000", "$382,000", "$418,000", "$407,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 220-10-45, comprehensive income = net income + OCI. Current-period OCI is $7,000: the $25,000 new unrealized gain less the $18,000 reclassification adjustment (gains moved from AOCI to net income upon sale, preventing double-counting). Total = $400,000 + $7,000 = $407,000. Option A ($425,000) omits the reclassification adjustment, double-counting the $18,000 realized gain in both net income and OCI.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Treasury shares excluded from dividend distribution
(2, 'Equity', 'Ferndale Corp. holds 5,000 shares of its own stock as treasury stock. The board declares a $2.00 per share cash dividend on its 100,000 issued shares of common stock. How many shares receive the dividend?', '["100,000", "5,000", "95,000", "105,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Treasury stock shares are issued but not outstanding — they do not receive dividends or carry voting rights under ASC 505-30-45. Only the 95,000 shares outstanding (100,000 issued minus 5,000 treasury) receive the $2.00 dividend. Option A (100,000) incorrectly includes treasury shares in the distribution, overstating the total dividend payment by $10,000.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Stock subscription receivable contra-equity classification
(2, 'Equity', 'Whitfield Corp. enters into stock subscription agreements for 10,000 shares of $5 par common stock at $22 per share. Subscribers make a 40% down payment, leaving an unpaid balance of $132,000. Under U.S. GAAP, how is the unpaid subscription receivable reported?', '["As a contra-equity account deducted from stockholders'' equity", "As a current asset under accounts receivable", "As a noncurrent asset under other receivables", "As a reduction of additional paid-in capital only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under SEC SAB Topic 4-E and prevailing U.S. GAAP practice per ASC 505-10-45, stock subscription receivables are presented as a contra-equity item deducted from stockholders'' equity, not as assets. The receivable represents an incomplete capital contribution rather than a third-party obligation. Option B is incorrect because presenting it as an asset would overstate both total assets and stockholders'' equity on the balance sheet.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Cash dividend liability arises at declaration date
(2, 'Equity', 'On which date does a cash dividend become a liability of the declaring corporation?', '["The ex-dividend date", "The declaration date", "The record date", "The payment date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 505-20-25-1, a cash dividend becomes a legal obligation on the declaration date, when the board formally authorizes payment. The entity debits Retained Earnings and credits Dividends Payable at that point. Option D (payment date) is when the liability is settled, not when it arises — the obligation exists from declaration through payment.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Statement of stockholders' equity purpose
(2, 'Equity', 'What is the primary purpose of the statement of stockholders'' equity?', '["To report the fair market value of all outstanding equity securities", "To disclose the tax effects of equity transactions", "To present the liquidation value available to each class of shareholder", "To reconcile beginning and ending balances of each equity component"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 505-10-50, the statement of stockholders'' equity reconciles the opening and closing balances of each equity component — common stock, APIC, retained earnings, treasury stock, and AOCI — showing all changes during the period. It captures stock issuances, dividends, net income, OCI, and treasury stock transactions. Option A is incorrect because equity accounts are carried at historical amounts, not fair value.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Book value per share with preferred liquidation preference and arrears
(2, 'Equity', 'Oakmont Corp. has 200,000 common shares ($5 par) outstanding and 10,000 shares of $100 par, 8% cumulative preferred stock with a liquidation preference of $105 per share. Dividends are two years in arrears. Total stockholders'' equity is $3,500,000. What is the book value per share of common stock?', '["$12.25", "$17.50", "$11.45", "$12.95"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Book value per common share allocates total equity to preferred first at their liquidation preference plus cumulative arrears. Preferred claim: $1,050,000 (10,000 × $105 liquidation) + $160,000 (10,000 × $100 × 8% × 2 years) = $1,210,000. Common equity: $3,500,000 − $1,210,000 = $2,290,000 ÷ 200,000 = $11.45. Option A ($12.25) omits dividends in arrears under ASC 505-10-50, understating the preferred shareholders'' total claim.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — ASU 2016-09 forfeiture policy election (actual forfeitures)
(2, 'Equity', 'Kendrick Corp. grants 12,000 RSUs with a three-year cliff vesting period and a grant-date fair value of $15 per RSU. The company elects to recognize forfeitures as they occur per ASU 2016-09. In Year 1, 600 RSUs are forfeited. No other forfeitures occur. What is compensation expense recognized in Year 1?', '["$57,000", "$60,000", "$51,000", "$54,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASU 2016-09 (codified in ASC 718-10-35), entities may elect to account for forfeitures as they occur rather than estimating them. With 600 RSUs forfeited in Year 1, expense is based on the 11,400 remaining RSUs: (11,400 × $15) ÷ 3 = $57,000. Option B ($60,000) ignores the Year 1 forfeitures, calculating expense on all 12,000 RSUs as if none had been forfeited during the period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Retained earnings deficit meaning
(2, 'Equity', 'What does a debit balance in the retained earnings account indicate?', '["The corporation''s stock is trading below par value", "The corporation has accumulated more losses than profits since inception", "The corporation has more liabilities than assets", "The corporation has declared more stock dividends than cash dividends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A debit balance in retained earnings, called a deficit, means cumulative net losses and dividends declared have exceeded cumulative net income since inception under ASC 505-10-45. This does not mean the company is insolvent. Option C is incorrect because a company can have negative retained earnings but positive total equity from contributed capital — a deficit reflects earnings history, not the asset-to-liability relationship.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Detachable warrants proportional allocation of proceeds
(2, 'Equity', 'Aldridge Corp. issues 5,000 units for $55 each, with each unit consisting of one share of preferred stock and one detachable stock warrant. After issuance, the preferred stock trades at $48 and the warrants trade at $12. Using the proportional method, how much of the $275,000 total proceeds is allocated to the warrants?', '["$60,000", "$12,000", "$48,000", "$55,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 470-20-25, when detachable warrants are issued with other securities, proceeds are allocated using the proportional (relative fair value) method. Total fair value per unit = $48 + $12 = $60. Warrant allocation: ($12 ÷ $60) × $275,000 = $55,000; preferred: ($48 ÷ $60) × $275,000 = $220,000. Option A ($60,000) uses the aggregate market value of warrants (5,000 × $12) rather than the proportional share of actual proceeds received.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — AFS sale reclassification — net zero comprehensive income effect
(2, 'Equity', 'Stonegate Corp. sells AFS debt securities for $180,000. The securities had an amortized cost of $150,000 and a cumulative unrealized gain of $30,000 previously recorded in AOCI. What is the net effect of this sale on total comprehensive income?', '["$0", "$30,000 increase", "$60,000 increase", "$30,000 decrease"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 320-10-35, when AFS securities are sold, the $30,000 realized gain enters net income. Simultaneously, the $30,000 previously in AOCI is reclassified out as a negative OCI adjustment. Net income increases $30,000 while OCI decreases $30,000, producing zero net effect on comprehensive income. Option B ($30,000 increase) double-counts the gain by including it in net income without recognizing the offsetting AOCI reclassification.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Equity method investor records share of investee OCI
(2, 'Equity', 'Stratford Corp. owns 40% of Windham Inc. and applies the equity method. During 2025, Windham reports net income of $500,000 and an unrealized loss of $60,000 on AFS debt securities in other comprehensive income. How does Stratford record its share of Windham''s OCI?', '["Debit Investment in Windham $24,000 and credit Other Comprehensive Income $24,000", "No entry is required because only the investor''s share of net income is recognized", "Debit Other Comprehensive Income $24,000 and credit Investment in Windham $24,000", "Debit Loss on Investment $24,000 and credit Investment in Windham $24,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 323-10-35-18, an equity method investor records its proportionate share of the investee''s OCI in its own other comprehensive income. Stratford debits OCI for $24,000 (40% × $60,000 loss) and credits the Investment account to reflect the decrease in Windham''s net equity. Option B is incorrect because the equity method requires recognition of all investee equity changes, including OCI items — not just net income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Multiple equity transactions — net effect on total stockholders' equity
(2, 'Equity', 'During 2025, Montclair Corp. completes the following: (1) issues 20,000 shares of $2 par common stock at $35 per share, (2) declares and pays $150,000 in cash dividends, (3) declares a 5% stock dividend when 220,000 shares are outstanding at $38 market price, (4) repurchases 8,000 shares at $40 using the cost method, and (5) resells 3,000 treasury shares at $45 per share. What is the net effect on total stockholders'' equity?', '["$515,000 increase", "$365,000 increase", "$783,000 increase", "$285,000 increase"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 505, only external transactions change total equity. Stock issuance: +$700,000. Cash dividends: −$150,000. Stock dividend: $0 (reclassification within equity). Treasury purchase: −$320,000 (8,000 × $40). Treasury resale: +$135,000 (3,000 × $45). Net = +$365,000. Option C ($783,000) incorrectly treats the stock dividend''s $418,000 fair value (11,000 shares × $38) as an increase rather than an internal equity reclassification.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
